    <body>
      <sec id="article-29913.s1" sec-type="Definition/Introduction">
        <title>Definition/Introduction</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">Telemedicine involves the use of telecommunication technologies to provide medical information and services. The&#x000a0;term&#x000a0;telemedicine is derived from the Greek root tele-, meaning "distant." The application of telemedicine principles to dermatology is called teledermatology. Teledermatology typically has been classified into 2 subtypes: real-time (synchronous) and store-and-forward (asynchronous).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29913.s2" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>Teledermatology has grown in potential over the past 2 decades. The US Department of Defense has been a pioneer in developing teledermatology protocols tailored for civilian programs. Real-time teledermatology involves a live video consultation with the patient, whereas store-and-forward teledermatology&#x000a0;relies on reviewing patient-submitted images to develop a diagnostic or management plan. Hybrid teledermatology combines elements of both of these approaches. Teledermatology conducted via mobile phones is referred to as mobile teledermatology. Extensions of teledermatology include teledermoscopy (consultation on dermoscopic images), teledermatopathology (review of dermatopathology slides), and telecytology (evaluation of cytology specimens). The inherently visual nature of dermatology makes it ideal for telemedicine.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Teledermatology is most commonly used for clinical management, patient consultation in remote locations, and medical support in nursing homes and home care settings, and is&#x000a0;practiced most widely in North America and Europe. Countries with lower clinician-to-patient ratios appear to be underrepresented in teledermatology practice. In general, telemedicine is considered to be cost-effective, although more rigorous studies are needed to fully examine its economic benefits.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Related Concepts</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Teledermoscopy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Dermoscopic patterns are well established for many dermatologic conditions, particularly skin malignancies. The combination of clinical teledermatology with teledermoscopy has been shown to improve the effectiveness of teledermatology consultations. Teledermoscopy enhances triage of teleconsultations for suspected malignancy. Direct, patient-initiated teledermoscopy is also effective for triage and early detection of skin cancer.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Teledermatopathology and telecytology&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Teledermatopathology and telecytology combine clinical data with high-quality supporting dermatopathology and cytology to enhance teledermatology's diagnostic capabilities. Teledermatopathology has become more feasible and effective because of advances in digitizing histopathology slides and the development of robot-assisted microscopy.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Teledermatology in the context of aesthetic medicine</bold>
</p>
        <p>The advantage of using teledermatology in aesthetic medicine is that it typically addresses conditions that are neither emergent nor life-threatening. Teledermatology can be employed for initial assessment and diagnosis of aesthetic conditions&#x000a0;as well as for follow-up care, particularly postprocedure, such as monitoring&#x000a0;patients who have undergone laser resurfacing.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Smartphone teledermatology/mobile teledermatology</bold>
</p>
        <p>One limitation of early teledermatology was the relatively cumbersome and costly equipment required for data exchange. Advances in technology have improved the quality of clinical image capture, storage, and transmission using smartphones, which readily support remote data exchange. Smartphone-based teledermatology and teledermoscopy are effective for diagnosing and triaging dermatologic conditions, including skin malignancies. The ease of use also makes smartphones the ideal tool for patient-initiated teledermatology. A further extension of smartphone-based practice is the use of social media platforms to discuss dermatology cases, allowing more physicians to view and comment on clinical cases in both diagnostic and management contexts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Operational and Ethical Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Effective teledermatology requires good infrastructure on both sides of the consultation. In many remote areas, limited infrastructure and lack of access to image-capture devices and reliable internet connectivity reduce the quality of clinical data. All clinicians involved in teledermatology must have basic training in dermatologic imaging and nomenclature. A proper electronic medical record (EMR) system, including linked image storage, is also integral to teledermatology.</p>
        <p>The practice and extent of maintaining&#x000a0;EMRs&#x000a0;vary&#x000a0;considerably worldwide. Standardizing dermatologic imaging and related&#x000a0;EMRs would be an important part of improving the quality and effectiveness of teledermatology practices. Clarity regarding the legal and ethical issues pertaining to teledermatology,&#x000a0;particularly those related to the confidentiality of patient data, must be established globally. The referring site should obtain informed consent before capturing images and explain how the images will be stored, transmitted, and used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29913.s3" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Results from studies have shown high patient satisfaction and good diagnostic concordance between teledermatology and face-to-face consultations. Teledermatology can be an effective triage tool, reducing referral times, even for cutaneous malignancies, especially when combined with teledermoscopy. Although teledermatology is typically used in outpatient dermatology, it has also been shown to be an effective tool in inpatient settings. In addition, teledermatology has been useful in managing chronic skin diseases, including chronic ulcers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>The basic concepts of teledermatology have been applied effectively in dermatology education and residency training programs. The use of telemedicine can facilitate the involvement of expert faculty in cost-effective teaching initiatives. Teledermatology also provides educational benefits to participants, including primary care providers and dermatologists. In addition, teledermatology has proven useful for other applications, such as remote interpretation of patch tests.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Patient-Initiated&#x000a0;Teledermatology</bold>
</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;4 most common business models used in teledermatology practice include standard fee-for-service reimbursement from insurance, capitated service contracts, per-case service contracts, and direct-to-consumer services. Patient-initiated teledermatology, which allows patients to consult directly with physicians and facilitates follow-up care, has become increasingly popular, but evidence supporting its effectiveness is limited. Patient-initiated teledermatology also enables patients to actively manage their health through direct communication with dermatologists.</p>
        <p>As the quality of image-capture devices, especially smartphones, increases, the effectiveness of patient-initiated teledermatology&#x000a0;is expected to improve. Results from recent studies have shown that patients can take accurate, high-quality dermoscopic images at home, enabling them to combine skin self-examination with patient-initiated teledermoscopy. This, in turn, can improve the quality of teledermatology consultations; however, patients must understand standard imaging protocols for this method to be effective.</p>
        <p>The development of business models for patient-initiated teledermatology must consider financial reimbursement for consultants, as well as legal and ethical issues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Strong evidence shows that teledermatology is effective in earlier diagnosis and triage of skin diseases.&#x000a0;Still, clinicians need to consider regional legal frameworks while practicing teledermatology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Latest Updates and Future Directions</bold>
</p>
        <p>The incorporation of teledermoscopy into teledermatology consultations has been shown to improve diagnostic concordance and is likely to become standard practice. Patient-initiated teledermoscopy within direct-to-patient teledermatology is also expected to become more common. Direct-to-consumer teledermatology is already widely used and may become the most common form of teledermatology practice over time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>The biggest influence on teledermatology in the future will be increasing incorporation of artificial intelligence (AI) into workflows and triage.&#x000a0;The shift toward precision and tailored dermatology is likely to be reflected in teledermatology, where&#x000a0;AI has already facilitated faster triage and reduced the administrative burden of dermatologic consultations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;AI-powered chatbots have begun serving as the initial point of contact for teleconsultations. In the future, they may perform preliminary screening and diagnosis, with the dermatologist intervening later to confirm the diagnosis, guide further management, and focus on complex cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>The dermatologist&#x02019;s supervisory role in making final management decisions remains paramount, and it should be emphasized that artificial intelligence serves as a tool rather than the primary diagnostic modality. Wearable or home-based sensors that patients can use to monitor skin conditions represent an emerging area with significant potential to enhance teledermatology. Real-time monitoring of parameters such as wound healing or inflammatory markers could further expand its diagnostic and clinical capabilities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Similar to other surgical specialties, robot-assisted dermatosurgery may expand the applications of teledermatology. In addition, the use of AI-based analysis and interventions in aesthetic dermatology is increasing. These applications range from simple image analysis and severity scoring to guided laser or cosmetic procedures, and may eventually include telerobotic dermatosurgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Although internet capacity and technology have improved globally, access to teledermatology remains limited in economically disadvantaged regions. This issue is particularly important, as populations with fewer resources stand to benefit the most from teledermatology services. Adoption of satellite-based technologies may help address these disparities and expand access in underserved areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29913.r23">[23]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29913.s4" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>Training clinicians and allied health workers to identify basic skin lesions, along with the incorporation of AI-based diagnostic and triage tools, could improve the quality and accessibility of teledermatology services in areas with a shortage of trained dermatologists.</p>
      </sec>
